<h1>The number system, in every key</h1>
<p class="lede">The number system names chords by their place in a key instead of by letter: a 1, a 4 and a 5 rather than C, F and G.
Every major key has the same seven chords in the same order, so once you know the row below you can play a song in any key.
This page builds it up step by step — where the row comes from, every key, the shapes that play it, and the circle of fifths that joins the keys — and ends with exercises to make it stick.
The diagrams switch between guitar and piano with the toggle at the top.</p>

<div class="spine" id="spine"></div>
<p class="spinenote">Read it as one word: major · minor · minor · major · major · minor · diminished.
Nashville charts write the numbers on the left; classical theory writes the Roman numerals underneath — same thing.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Where the row comes from</h2>
<p>Start from a major scale — in C, the white keys C D E F G A B — and number its notes 1 to 7.
To build a chord on any of them, take that note, skip one, take the next, skip one, take the next: C&ndash;E&ndash;G on the 1, D&ndash;F&ndash;A on the 2, E&ndash;G&ndash;B on the 3, and so on.
The major scale always has its half steps in the same places, so the stacks always come out the same way:
major on 1, 4 and 5, minor on 2, 3 and 6, and a squeezed, diminished chord on 7.
Change key and every letter changes, but the numbers and their qualities stay put. That's why players talk in numbers:
“1&ndash;5&ndash;6m&ndash;4” means the same thing in every key, while “C&ndash;G&ndash;Am&ndash;F” only works in C. The first row of the table below is this exact list, in C.</p>


